                            COWABUNGA DELETER
                            by Mack Lifeguard

                               INTRODUCTION
                               ~~~~~~~~~~~~
Congratulations!  You have just acquired the most comprehensive,
sophisticated file deleter for Amiexpress.  It is fast, easy to use, and
very powerful.  It is the culmination of many hours of work by the Dual
Crew-Shining team:  the leader in quality, professional software for your
Amiexpress system.

When I first set out to code this utility, I had three goals in mind:
innovative features, simple controls, and speed; the same philosophy behind
Amiexpress.

I am happy to say that Cowabunga Deleter (hereafter written as CD) has
achieved all three goals.

So why another deleter you ask? The auto-deleter market is saturated enough
as it is, but what one deleter offers, the other lacks.  They are either
too obscure and difficult to control, or too simple and missing important
features.

I have changed all that in CD. I implemented traditional deleting
features, along with new, innovative ones, such as the keyword search and
date-range match.  These new features allow sysops to delete only certain
files. The days of massive wares sacrifices are over!

If you are looking for a CLI-based deleter that wakes up every morning and
aimlessly deletes all the files in your HD, then CD is not for you.  The
intended user of CD is that sysop who likes to know exactly what goes on in
his board, including file maintenance.  If files are to be deleted then he
will want to specify exactly which ones, not just a random 30 Meg
genocidal wipeout (but don't worry, CD offers this feature too!).  Perhaps
the sysop would like to keep some of his wares, but wants all the betas
and previews zapped.  Currently, CD is the only deleter that will let you
do this.

So read on, mate, and start deleting the smart way, the Cowabunga way!


                          INSTALLATION PROCEDURES
                          ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Copy "Cowbung" to your C: directory.
Copy "Cowbung.cfg" to your S: directory.
Copy "reqtools.library" to your LIBS: directory.

CD requires (v38) of the reqtools.library. If you don't have it, I have
included it within this archive. It's PD, so I don't think Nico will mind.



                               MATCHING LOGIC
                               ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Files can be matched a total of 8 different ways:
* Marker Only
* Date Only
* Keyword(s) Only
* Marker & Date
* Marker & Keyword(s)
* Date & Keyword(s)
* Marker & Date & Keyword(s)
* No parameters: this will match ALL files with an x overall bytes ceiling.

First some definitions:

DIRECTORY LISTING: This is the DIR file contained in all /X conferences.

MARKER: A plain-jane ASCII character that preceeds a filename in the
DIR listing, like this: @ABCD.LHA P  12304  01-20-93....

DATE MATCH: The date of the filename as shown in the DIR list is compared
against the two dates that you, the sysop specify.

KEYWORD MATCH: Keywords specified by you, the sysop, are scanned in the
descriptions of each and every file in the DIR list. Case is not important
since all descriptions are internally converted to uppercase and so is the
keyword. The actual filename is not scanned, only the descriptions.

                                  --o--

When the program is matching by parameters, all parts have to be satisfied
with the logical AND operator to make a match.  That is, if you choose
Marker & Date, the file has to be marked with a special symbol AND fall
between a certain date range.  It is important to note that the logic used
here is not EITHER/OR (a keyword OR a marker OR a date.. this won't work).

Some of the early beta-testers of this program complained that they would
select MARKER & DATE & KEYWORD but it wouldn't match the files they
wanted.  That is because in this example, a file and its description MUST
meet all three criteria.  If it meets 2 out of 3, it will not be matched.
I don't know of an easier way to explain this.

If you choose Marker & Date & Keyword(s), then all three parameters must be
satisfied: the file must have a marker, be within a date range, and have at
least one matching keyword in its description. If you specify Marker &
Date, then the file must have a marker and be within a date. If you choose
Marker only, then it just needs a marker; its date is ignored.

Choosing only one parameter will match files that satisfy that parameter
only, the rest are not examined.

If you do not choose any of the 3 parameters, then all files in the DIR
will be matched for deletion.  You will be prompted for a maximum number
of bytes to be deleted.  Once x bytes have been deleted, then the deletion
process is halted.  This is WIPEOUT mode, and should be used with care
since it will not discriminate and go on a rampant file-killing spree.

This may sound confusing at first, so I recommend a few trial runs with
CD's unique PRETEND mode, that way you will get an idea of what is matched
and what isn't.

I cannot go into detail for every possible combination of all features,
but please, don't let this matching business discourage you, I can assure
you that once you have understood its simple, but sophisticated logic, you
will never put this tool down.


                            GADGETS & OPERATION
                            ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
It is a good idea to view these instructions while running CD in the
background.

ADD (click gadget)
This is for selecting an Amiexpress DIR listing. It will automatically scan
for a valid PATHS file. You can select an unlimited number of directory
listings. Each one will be processed in ascending order (first the bottom
file, then up its way to the top file, removing each entry as it goes.)
Clicking the right mouse button at the file requester will bring up a list
of devices. Using the combination SHIFT-RETURN will move the cursor from
the directory string to the filename string and so on.

DELETE (click gadget)
If you have selected a directory with the ADD gadget, you can delete it
from the listing with this gadget.  Just click on the one(s) you want to
remove.  There is no limit to the number of selections, use the scrollbar
if you have to.


LOG ACTIVE  (checkmark gadget)
This sets the flag for writing to a CD log.  You can call it whatever you
like, and place it wherever you want.  If one is not present, it will be
created.  You can specify a non-existent directory within the file
requester, and it will be created for you.  Hitting return at the
requester will set the default log to RAM:COWBUNG.LOG.  I strongly
recommend having a log, as it will report any errors encountered during
the deletion process.


OVERWRITE FILESIZE  (checkmark gadget)
When a file is deleted, you can specify a word to replace the bytes in the
description.  A requester will pop up and ask you for the word.  Entering a
blank or cancelling the requester will default to "OFFLINE".  For example:

         AEXPR_1.DMS  P 390361  12-10-92  Art Expression Disk 1/4.
                                  becomes:
         AEXPR_1.DMS  P OFFLINE 12-10-92  Art Expression Disk 1/4.

This feature, like all description-modifying ones, is only effective if
the file was deleted, otherwise its description will not be harmed.


MOVE DESCRIPTIONS  (checkmark gadget)
Many times it is useful to move unwanted descriptions of deleted files to
a purge file.  When you activate this gadget, a file-requester will prompt
you for the destination file.  NOTE:  if you selected more than one DIR,
all of the matched descriptions will be moved to this destination file.


KILL DESCRIPTIONS  (checkmark gadget)
Once a file has been succesfully deleted from the drive, this option can
delete the description of it too.  Very handy for keeping a clean DIR
listing. This gadget will be ghosted if the MOVE gadget is checked.
Converesely, the MOVE and OVERWRITE gadgets will be ghosted when this box
is checked. In other words, you can't move a description and kill it too!


MATCH MARKED FILES  (checkmark gadget)
Here you set the character to be used as a marker.  I recommend using the
"@" symbol.  Pressing return or cancelling the requester will default the
marker to "@".  Files will only be deleted if they match the marker, and
if this gadget is checked.  If you have not checked the KILL DESCRIPTIONS
gadget, then the marker will be removed from the filename once it has been
deleted.  This is to avoid future attempts at deleting an old marked file.
For example:

@AEXPR_1.DMS  P 390361  12-10-92  Art Expression Disk 1/4,
^
|_____marker goes here!


MATCH DATE RANGE   (checkmark gadget)
When you activate this gadget, the bottom left string gadgets become
unghosted, allowing you to input the TO and FROM date ranges. Any file
having a date that is between or equal to these dates will be deleted.
You can enter a very old date and a current date, for example, if you want
all files deleted up to that current date. Something like:
                        01-01-01   TO   07-20-93.
This will ensure all files up to 07-20-93 are zapped.

You can also enter just one day by using identical dates. Like this:
                        07-20-93   TO   07-20-93
That will delete only files uploaded on that day.

Remember, only files that fall between or directly ON the dates you
specify will be deleted.  Obviously, any dates in the future will be
ignored and no files matched.


MATCH KEYWORD(S)  (checkmark gadget)
This allows you to enter up to 100 words (or 900 characters) to be used
as matching keys. That is, a file description will be scanned for these keys
and if any are found, the file will be deleted.  You can use this to delete
all of your "previews betas demos" etc.  The keys are not case sensitive, so
type them in as you like; just separate them with a space, comma, or both.
Like this: "Preview, BeTa, demo text ansi, ascii, collection" (without the
quotes!). Notice how the comma is optional, and so is case.

This is CD's most powerful option, so please be careful.  Sometimes you
will match more files than expected.  For example, the keywords:

                  Sent By: hackerman, Sent by: lamerdude

Would match ALL of your descriptions, since they all have "Sent" on them.
That's assuming you have that "Sent by:" option, but you get the point.
Remember, each keyword is sovereign and checked for matches independently
of other keywords.


FOR REAL/PRETEND (cycle gadget)
If you want to test drive CD, cycle this gadget to PRETEND mode.  PRETEND
mode is a very clever feature that allows the skeptic sysop to get a taste
of CD's power.  All of the specified flags are used, the file is searched
and matched, but the actual deletion does not take place.  The log will
show "Pretended to delete:  <filename>".  No descriptions will be altered
even if you have the Overwrite Bytes flag set.  Nothing is changed.

        *********************************************************
        **  This option is highly recommended for CD rookies.  **
        *********************************************************


GO DELETE  (click gadget)
This gadget is ghosted until a valid DIR has been entered into the scan
list.  When this gadget is clicked, and before the deletion process begins,
you will get a requester showing you all of the deleting parameters.  You
can, at that time, abort and make changes.  Once the process begins, it
cannot be aborted!

WIPOUT MODE  (special circumstancial requester)
This option is not within the CD window.  It is a requester that is
brought up only if none of the 3 matching parameters have been selected
and GO DELETE has been clicked.  Without any matching conditions, all
files will be deleted, hence some sort of control is necessary.  You will
be prompted for a maximum number of bytes that are to be deleted, once the
deleting engine reaches that amount of deleted bytes, it will stop. If you
have PRETEND mode activated, you will notice in the log that all
files in the DIR list were "pretended to delete". This is normal. During
regular execution, no files after x deleted bytes will be harmed.

    ***Be careful with this option, it is for advanced CD users only.***


REQTOOLS REQUESTERS:  These special requesters (the only ones in the
program), are called from the reqtools.library ç, © by Nico François.
They all have similar features.  Pressing return will automatically choose
the OK gadget, pressing Amiga-X will delete the text in the string,
Amiga-v will choose OK, and Amiga-B will cancel.  Sometimes you may find
that you cannot enter any text into a requester.  This is because there is
already a default value in there and the requester is set to only grab X
number of characters.  Simply use the backspace or delete key and start
over.

                                  TUTORIAL
                                  ~~~~~~~~
First, crank up CD and have her running in the background while you read
this manual.  The first thing you will do is click on the FOR REAL gadget
(bottom right of display), until it reaches PRETEND mode.  This is very
important!  If you somehow make a mistake, your files will not be harmed.

If you are using the preset configuration file, then some box gadgets will
be checkmarked. Go ahead and turn them all OFF. There shouldn't be any
checkmarked gadgets.

Click on the LOG ACTIVE gadget until it is checkmarked. You will be prompted
for a filename. Hit return at this time, and the default RAM:COWBUNG.LOG
will be used.

This tutorial is divided in 3 sections:  Marker Match, Date Match, Keyword
Match, and Marker & Date & Keyword.


MARKER Match
°°°°°°°°°°°°
Go into your favorite text editor and load up a directory listing from
any conference.  Go to the very top of your list and attach the symbol '@'
to a file.  It should look something like this:

@AEXPR_1.DMS  P 390361  12-10-92  Art Expression Disk 1/4,
^
|____notice the '@' marker!

Now, save it and go back to CD.

Click on the MATCH MARKED FILES gadget until it appears checkmarked.

You will be prompted to enter a character to be used as a marker. You can
either enter '@' (without the quotes!), or simply hit return ('@' is the
default setting). Sometimes, you may have to hit backspace/delete because
the requester only allows 1 character to be entered.

Ok, we're ready to go. Hit the ADD gadget and select the directory you just
modified earlier.

If the file was a valid DIR listing, the the GO DELETE button will become
unghosted.

Click on the GO DELETE button.

A requester will pop up showing you a list of parameters. Make sure they
match the following:

Log File: RAM:COWBUNG.LOG
Overwrite File Size: NO
Move Descriptions: NO
Kill Descriptions: NO
Match Marked Files: '@'
Match Date Range: NO
Match Keywords: NO
Pretend Delete: YES     <---Be sure this is set to "YES"
Wipeout Bytes: 0

Once you have checked that everything is OK, hit return.  If something is
not right, just click on ABORT and fix it.

Ok, once CD has finished doing its thing, come back to the editor and load
up "RAM:COWBUNG.LOG".

Pretty nice, eh? Very detail-oriented log, straight out of Westpoint!

You should see that the file you added the '@' marker was not deleted,
just pretend-delete.  And at the bottom of the log, in the SUMMARY, it
should indicate that it matched 1 file and deleted 0 bytes and 0 files.

End of Marker tutorial.

DATE Match
°°°°°°°°°°
This tutorial will be written at a later time.


                            THEORY OF OPERATION
                            ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
CD was written in optimized SAS C 6.3.  Two versions are available, one
for 68000 machines, and the other for 68020+.  The search routine is
written by NZO/DCS and can match keywords at 140,000 CPS (40% faster than
Cygnus Editor).

The program is fully gadget-driven and uses the reqtools.library by Nico
François.

When an Amiexpress DIR listing is selected and GO DELETE is hit, the
program will look for the DIR's corresponding PATHS file, which is located
within the same branch.  Then it will scan the list looking for a match
based on the specified parameters.  Once it has matched and deleted a
file, it will be posted to the log (if selected), and its description will
be modified, removed, or left alone.  If the file was not deleted for some
reason (erased, write-protected, etc.), then no changes will be made to
its description and an error will be posted to the CD log (if selected).

At the end, if any files were deleted the new modified DIR is saved to
disk.  If a log was selected, it will be saved too.  This saves a
considerable amount of time and is why CD is so fast.  Other deleters
modify listings as they go, whereas CD stores a buffer in memory for its
alternate DIRs and logs and does not interfere with the deletion process.

For more information on the matching conditions, read the MATCHING LOGIC
section of this manual.


